# Who reviews what. GitHub requests a review from the owners of every path a
# pull request touches; with "Require review from Code Owners" on in branch
# protection, their approval is what merges it.
#
# The default owner covers everything. The paths below it are the ones that
# decide who can do what — sign-in, sessions, roles, scopes, tenancy, rate
# limits, the error filter that shapes every response, the browser's
# security headers, CI's tokens — and get a second look whatever else the
# change is. A project made from this starter replaces the handle with its
# own, or a team (`@org/security`) on the critical lines.

*                                   @JordiParraCrespo

# Authentication and sessions
/packages/auth/                     @JordiParraCrespo
/apps/api/src/auth/                 @JordiParraCrespo

# Authorization: roles, policies, scopes
/packages/backend/authz/            @JordiParraCrespo
/apps/api/src/authz/                @JordiParraCrespo
/apps/api/src/roles/                @JordiParraCrespo
/packages/shared/src/permissions/   @JordiParraCrespo
/packages/shared/src/scopes/        @JordiParraCrespo

# flama:begin organizations
# Tenancy: what one organization can see of another
/apps/api/src/organizations/        @JordiParraCrespo
# flama:end organizations

# Abuse limits, error shaping, configuration and secrets handling
/apps/api/src/throttling/           @JordiParraCrespo
/packages/backend/core/src/filters/ @JordiParraCrespo
/apps/api/src/config/               @JordiParraCrespo

# flama:begin web
# The SPA's Content-Security-Policy and headers
/apps/web/nginx.conf                @JordiParraCrespo
# flama:end web
# flama:plugins codeowners

# What CI runs and with which token
/.github/                           @JordiParraCrespo
